   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Hola, the simplest hello\n- Picture this: hola, the simplest greeting, means hello.\n- Hola fits any time of day, even with people just met.\n- You can use it at any time of day, with friends or with people you have just met.\n- Takeaway: hola is the one-word hello for any hour.\n\n## Greetings for times of day\n- Next, step 1: buenos días for good morning.\n- Step 2: buenas tardes for good afternoon.\n- Step 3: buenas noches for good evening or good night.\n- Layout: buenos + días (masculine plural) | buenas + tardes, noches (feminine plural).\n- Think of it like drawing comics: the ending must match its panel, buenos with días, buenas with tardes and noches.\n- Takeaway: the ending changes to buenos with días, buenas with tardes and noches.\n\n## Saying goodbye\n- Next, step 1: adiós means goodbye.\n- Step 2: hasta luego means see you later.\n- Hasta luego is friendly, for someone you expect to see soon.\n- Think of it like a video-game rematch invite: hasta luego fits a soon return.\n- Takeaway: hasta luego is the friendly goodbye for a soon return.\n\n## Asking how someone is\n- Next, step 1: Spanish asks how someone is both informally and formally.\n- Step 2: cómo estás means how are you, used with friends, family, children.\n- Step 3: cómo está usted also means how are you, for older people or strangers.\n- Step 4: the usual answer is bien, gracias, meaning fine, thank you.\n- Takeaway: use cómo estás with friends, cómo está usted in polite situations.\n\n## Meeting someone for the first time\n- Then, step 1: when you first meet someone, say mucho gusto.\n- Mucho gusto means nice to meet you.\n- Takeaway: mucho gusto is the line for a first meeting.",
    "mirror": "## Hola, the simplest greeting\n- Hola means hello, and the text says you can use it at any time of day, with friends or with people you have just met.\n- Which single greeting does the text say works at any time of day and even with people you have just met?\n- The answer is hola, because the text calls it the simplest greeting and gives it no time-of-day limit at all.\n\n## Greetings for a time of day\n- Layout: buenos + días (masculine plural) → good morning | buenas + tardes (feminine plural) → good afternoon | buenas + noches (feminine plural) → good evening or good night.\n- The text pairs buenos días with good morning, buenas tardes with good afternoon, and buenas noches with good evening or good night.\n- Why does the ending shift from buenos to buenas when the noun changes from días to tardes or noches?\n- The ending changes because días is masculine plural while tardes and noches are feminine plural, so buenos and buenas follow the noun.\n- Explain in your own words why you write buenos días but buenas tardes, because the text links each ending to the noun's gender and plural number.\n\n## Saying goodbye\n- Adiós means goodbye, and hasta luego means see you later, which the text describes as a friendly way to say goodbye.\n- Which of the two goodbyes would you pick if you expect to see the person again soon, and why?\n- You would pick hasta luego, because the text says it is used when you expect to see the person again soon.\n\n## Asking how someone is\n- Use the informal cómo estás with friends, family and children, but use the formal cómo está usted with older people, strangers or polite situations.\n- Both questions mean how are you, and the usual answer the text gives is bien, gracias, which means fine, thank you.\n- Which form of the how-are-you question does the text give for strangers, older people or polite situations?\n- The formal cómo está usted fits those situations, because the text pairs it with older people, strangers and polite settings rather than friends.\n\n## Meeting someone for the first time\n- When you meet someone for the first time, the text says to use mucho gusto, which means nice to meet you.\n- What does the text tell you to say at a first meeting, and what does that phrase mean?\n- At a first meeting you say mucho gusto, which the text translates as nice to meet you in English.\n- Optional harder question: at night, how would you greet and politely ask an older stranger how they are, using only phrases from the text?"
