   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Militarism\n- Historians often describe four long-term causes of the First World War, plus one event that set it off; the first is militarism.\n- Before 1914 the great powers built up large armies and navies.\n- Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Think of the battleship race as two video game players upgrading characters, which pictures the material's point that Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Generals began to plan for war, and many leaders came to see it as a normal way to settle disputes.\n\n## Alliances\n- The second cause is alliances.\n- Europe was divided into two blocs.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain, and the Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy.\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries.\n- Think of the alliance blocs as two basketball teams, where a clash can pull in both teams, picturing that a local conflict could pull in many countries.\n- A quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war because of these alliances.\n\n## Imperialism\n- The third cause is imperialism, which means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- The European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia.\n- This competition increased distrust between them.\n\n## Nationalism\n- The fourth cause is nationalism, strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- Think of nationalism as a comic artist making one nation the hero, picturing the material's point of strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans.\n- Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples in the Balkans.\n- Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n\n## The spark and the chain\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- The next step was Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia.\n- Because of the alliances, this war soon drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain.\n- The result is that a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- Harder question: Why could a local conflict pull in many countries?",
    "mirror": "## The set-up: four long-term causes plus a spark, starting with militarism\n- Historians usually name four long-term causes of World War I, plus one event that set it off.\n- Militarism is the first cause: before 1914 the great powers built big armies and navies, and Germany and Britain raced on battleships.\n- Generals planned for war, and many leaders came to see war as a normal way to settle disputes.\n- Think of it like a basketball league where every team keeps buying taller players and new gear — that's militarism, the build-up the text describes.\n\n## Cause two: alliances split Europe into two blocs\n- The second cause is alliances, and Europe was divided into two blocs.\n- Layout: two columns — Triple Entente: France, Russia, Britain | Triple Alliance: Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy.\n- Because of the alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries, so a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- Think of it like a video game team-up: hit one player and their whole squad joins the fight, just as the alliances pulled many countries in.\n\n## Cause three: imperialism\n- Imperialism is the third cause, and it means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- The European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia, which increased distrust between them.\n- Think of it like comic artists racing to claim every panel and page on the shared table — that's the empires competing for colonies, markets and power.\n\n## Cause four: nationalism\n- Nationalism is the fourth cause: strong pride in one's nation, or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans: Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples, and Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n\n## The spark: the assassination and the chain that followed\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- Because of the alliances, Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia soon drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain.\n- Layout: 28 June 1914, Sarajevo → Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia → Russia drawn in → Germany → France → Britain."
